72
00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.960
These technical vulnerabilities translate
directly to the physical world.

73
00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.560
We are looking at active, mobile security
failures currently walking

74
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.000
through public spaces.

75
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.720
Right now, unpatched surveillance
platforms with known encrypted exfiltration channels

76
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.600
are disproportionately patrolling
low-income public housing and heavily policed residential

77
00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.560
neighborhoods. Exploiting these CVEs gives
an attacker live audio, high-definition

78
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.400
video, and a mobile physical platform
operating inside a citizen's

79
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.920
common space. In AI safety, failure cannot
be treated as

80
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.320
a residual edge case to be patched later.

81
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.240
We have to invert our approach.

82
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.560
We must characterize the failures first,
treating safety tests with

83
00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.560
the exact same rigor as a structural
engineer's load test.

84
00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.960
Before we scale the next generation of
autonomous machines, regulators

85
00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.920
must abandon surface-level marketing
audits.

86
00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.880
We need legally mandated process layer
security, coordinated vulnerability disclosures,

87
00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:47.080
and strict procurement bans on foreign
backdoors.

88
00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.200
The technology to secure these systems
exists.

89
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.600
We just have to choose to enforce it.

